WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.760
- If the Thames Barrier were
to fail when it was needed,

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.350
the damage would be almost
impossible to calculate.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.520
The best estimates I've found say that
the financial cost of London flooding

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.730
would be somewhere between
£20 billion and £100 billion.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:13.840
The Houses of Parliament,

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.000
every skyscraper in the city of London,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.040
every building near the river
would be unusable for weeks.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.660
The central parts of
the London Underground

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.800
would be out of action
for months, possibly more.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.010
That's before you get to the human costs.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.350
Worst case, perhaps hundreds
of thousands of people

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.430
who'd be forced out of their homes.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.120
Which is why this barrier
is designed to not fail.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.660
To be clear, that still means it can
have occasional glitches

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:40.960
or scheduled maintenance, or even
have parts of it break entirely.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.650
But if any of those things happen,
it must still protect London.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.350
And there's a whole team
who work here full time

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.240
to make sure that it does.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.270
- We aim to move each of
the ten individual gates

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.140
at least twice a month to exercise them.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.940
Today is one of our monthly test closures.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.210
For test closures, we close just as
the tide is finishing going out.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.410
And that way, we can
wait until the tide turns

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.300
and then we get equilibrium
on both sides of the Barrier,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.990
and therefore, we can reopen.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:15.600
That avoids us having to be
closed for the full tidal cycle.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.510
- A couple of months ago,

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.160
the Barrier had a full
high-tide test closure

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.260
and it is dramatic.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.735
Normally, the gates
just sit out of the way

00:01:21.735 --> 00:01:22.568
on the bottom of the river,

00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:25.780
but when they're needed,
they're rotated up into place.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.520
- Well, for the annual test closure,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.900
because we're trying to
maximise the differential,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.510
the water levels on each
side of the barrier,

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.100
we like to close early in the tidal cycle

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.060
to allow that differential
level to build up

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.940
to a maximum of 4½ metres.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.340
On a flood defence closure,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.070
we close much later in the tidal cycle,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:48.074
because we just want to stop
the danger element of the tide 

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:49.990
going up into Central London.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:51.980
We try and be gentle with the river.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.610
So, we start by closing
the outer smaller gates.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.270
And then, we slowly work
across into the middle.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.300
And sometimes, we'll bring
the two 61-metre gates

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.020
in the centre of the river
up a couple of metres below the surface,

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.800
before we finally bring them
into the closed position.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.780
And that avoids a
reflective wave of the water

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.234
that's coming in and being stopped,

00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:15.460
being pushed back downstream.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.200
So, during a flood defence closure,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.530
we don't have to stop the whole tide
going into Central London.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.930
We just need to stop the part of that tide

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.580
that would overtop the linear defences.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.870
Normal spring tides that
go into London each day

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.210
do not overtop the embankment
walls, for example.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.800
But if you've got a surge
element on top of that,

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.790
that is what would go over the top
of the walls in London,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.650
and that's the element that we
need to stop with the Barrier.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.280
Once we've stopped the
danger element of the tide

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.580
and got a differential,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.590
we've effectively created a reservoir
upstream of the Barrier.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.440
We can then over-rotate the large gates

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.220
and allow a controlled
flow of water upstream

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.170
into the reservoir that we've created.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.340
And again, that reduces the amount of time

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.600
before we get equilibrium on both sides
of the barrier and can reopen.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.050
- The barrier cannot be allowed
to fail when it's needed.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.210
So, each gate can be moved by
one of four separate motors.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.490
That service tunnel I was in?
There are two of them,

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.250
a few metres apart, identical,

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.220
with completely independent
power and control systems.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.120
The barrier has three connections
to the UK power grid,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.060
for both the north and south of the river.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.660
And if the entire British
national power grid fails,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.470
which has never happened, but if it does,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.500
the barrier has three
separate diesel generators,

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.910
any one of which could
operate the entire thing.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.950
- We have a number of modes of operation,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.520
so that if, when we're
using Mode 1 operation,

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.390
we have a problem of a technical nature,
we go to Mode 2.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.360
Problem with Mode 2,
we go to Mode 3.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.890
We don't necessarily have time
to sort out any technical issues,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.800
but we've got other modes of operation
that we can fall back on.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.790
The barrier has to close
when we need it to.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.800
I always say that if the Prime Minister

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.960
was stood in the House of
Commons in his wellingtons

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.310
and we said, "Sorry, PM,
we'll get it right next tide,"

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.870
that would probably be
fairly career-limiting(!)

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.880
- So, that's the technical resilience.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.750
What about the physical construction?

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.330
Well, these piers are
made of Portland cement,

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.440
so, they'll stand for a
couple of centuries at least.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.450
The steel gates are protected by
what's called a sacrificial anode.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.400
Corrosion in water like this
is an electrochemical process.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.650
So, if you connect a chunk of metal

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.170
that's more easily corroded
to something like the gate,

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:22.150
that chunk of metal will take
the corrosion damage instead,

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.570
and you can replace it fairly
easily whenever you need to.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.466
So, the gates will also be
fine for a century or so.

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:29.830
What if a ship hits the Barrier?

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.810
Well, ships are designed to float.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.170
Relatively speaking,
they're not that solid.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:35.740
These piers are thick concrete,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.516
and each of the main gates
is 3,000 tonnes of steel.

00:04:38.516 --> 00:04:40.570
So, the ship's going to come off worse.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.580
Collisions have actually
happened several times.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.380
In 1997, a ship called the
Sand Kite hit one of the piers.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.310
The only damage to the Barrier
was some scratched paint

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.120
and a broken ladder, but the ship sank.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.930
The trouble was that it sank where it hit

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.580
and sat broken on top of
one of the open gates.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.320
That would've blocked that
part of the Barrier from closing.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.770
The contingency plan,
if there had been a flood tide,

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:03.020
would have been to close
all the other gates

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.290
and use the force of the
water trying to get through

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.133
to blast the wrecked ship clear.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.760
But also, even if one gate
was completely broken somehow,

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.610
the Barrier would still
not technically fail.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.520
The margin for error would be gone,
but that's why the margin's there.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.630
It would still hold back enough
water to keep London safe.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.340
- We have a series of forecast models

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.590
and we run what we call
an ensemble forecast

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.280
that looks many tides ahead.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.040
With any models,
particularly forecast models,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.350
you will always get a degree of error.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:38.210
So, the forecaster here will make
an adjustment to the forecast

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.420
and that data will be
passed to the controller

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:44.070
from which to make a decision
as to whether to close or not.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.980
- So, the barrier is
highly reliable and strong.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.620
What if there's a really high tide,

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.830
a storm surge that's too
much for this to cope with?

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.170
Well, the design odds of that
are one in a thousand per year.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.360
Not technically failure, just not
being specified high enough.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.264
The barrier was designed
in 1970 for a 60-year life,

00:05:59.264 --> 00:06:00.980
which would be 2030,

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:03.380
which must've seemed a
very long way away then.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:05.950
The good news is that with
modern simulations and modelling,

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:08.920
the team here are confident that
it'll last another 40 years at least.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.100
But part of the reason for that is
because those 1970s engineers

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.230
built in a huge margin of safety.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:17.830
They didn't cut corners then, and
that's given us breathing space now.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:19.410
- The gates themselves,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.150
with inspections and remedial works,

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.250
will probably be good to go until 2070,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.250
but a lot of the
supporting infrastructure,

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:32.000
the fire systems, the security systems,
even the cranes on the piers

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.270
will all have to be updated
in the years to come.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:39.140
- But what if the tide went high enough
to actually overtop the barrier?

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.040
Well, those gates are built strong enough
that any water they can't hold back

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.040
can flow under into the
low water on the other side.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.160
Remember, they close the barrier early,
when the river's at its lowest point,

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.750
so, yes, if some apocalyptic
storm comes along,

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:53.900
the barrier should still work well enough.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.540
If this was almost anything else,

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.960
you could say the redundancy
was over-specified,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.670
that requirements like
this were ridiculous,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.970
but with so much at stake,

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:04.470
the Thames Barrier is a marvel
not just of engineering,

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.653
but of how you have to build when
something cannot be allowed to fail.

